Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a string stream to hold the HTML output
    std::stringstream htmlStream;

    // Write the markdown text to the string stream
    htmlStream << markdown;

    // Return the HTML output as a string
    return htmlStream.str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a markdown text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to write the markdown text to HTML, which is then returned as a string. Note that this implementation does not handle all markdown syntax, so it may not be suitable for all markdown documents.

If you need a more robust solution, you might consider using a markdown parser library like `libmarkdown` or `markdown-cpp`. These libraries can handle a wider range of markdown syntax and produce more accurate HTML output.

